---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/foundational/verification-before-publication">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Journalism. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only a
    habit of asking where information comes from. Everything else is built here, step by
    step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what journalism actually sells — not claims, but the chain of checks behind claims — and why the checks must happen <em>before</em> publication;</li>
    <li>use the news values editors weigh when deciding what is worth covering, and read the motive behind any source;</li>
    <li>explain why first reports so often get things wrong, and what stage of the information lifecycle you are looking at;</li>
    <li>write in the inverted pyramid and treat attribution as honesty about how much you know;</li>
    <li>run an interview that collects and tests information at the same time, and apply the two-source rule without fooling yourself;</li>
    <li>say what sub-editors and editors check before a story prints, and how a publish-or-hold decision is made;</li>
    <li>check any claim you meet as a reader, using the same four moves professional fact-checkers use.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a claim appears, we ask how it could be checked before asking
    whether to believe it. The checking is the journalism.
  </p>

  <h2>The street-corner problem</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine two people standing on a street corner telling you opposite stories about a car
    crash that happened there. You have no special tools — only questions. Whom did you see?
    Were you facing the road or your phone? Have you told this story to anyone else before
    me? Asking those questions well <em>is</em> journalism. In a world where anyone can
    publish a claim in three seconds, the newsroom's product is not the claim — it is the
    chain of checks behind the claim. Anyone can be a publisher; a professional is someone
    whose assertions come with an audit trail. That is why journalism's entire value in an
    infinite-content world reduces to one verb: <strong>verified</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters because of a quirk of human psychology: information does not need to be
    true to be useful to the person spreading it. A rumour can get a click, sell a product
    or win an election regardless of its accuracy. The only durable defence is a discipline
    applied <em>before</em> publication — because a correction afterwards, however prominent,
    rarely reaches everyone the original claim reached. Think of verification as the
    cooking, not the garnish: once a false story is served, you cannot un-serve it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet first — the correction that came too late" widget="predict" config={vpCorrectionPredict} />
  <p>
    The five checks below run through this whole lesson like a spine. Each section that
    follows takes one part of the method apart.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five checks every claim must survive" widget="equationStep" config={verifyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps are ordered by cost, cheapest first. Finding
    the original source takes seconds and kills most false claims on its own; seeking
    comment can take days; publishing with uncertainty is the discipline that keeps the
    rest honest. Notice also what is absent — no step says "check whether it feels true".
    Feelings are exactly what a well-made rumour is designed to move.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> journalism's value is a chain of checks, ordered from cheapest
    to most expensive. But before anything can be checked, someone must decide it is worth
    checking.
  </p>

  <h2>What is news: finding and assessing stories</h2>
  <p>
    Editors use a loose but real checklist of <strong>news values</strong> — the qualities
    that make an event worth reporting: <strong>impact</strong> (how many people, how
    deeply), <strong>proximity</strong> (near you in space or concern), <strong>prominence</strong>
    (famous names attract attention), <strong>timeliness</strong> (why print it today),
    <strong>conflict</strong>, <strong>novelty</strong> (the classic "man bites dog"), and
    <strong>human interest</strong>. A curriculum change affecting 40,000 pupils usually
    outranks a celebrity haircut, even though the haircut might get more clicks — and the
    tension between "important" and "attention-grabbing" is a daily editorial negotiation,
    not a solved problem.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the stories — public importance or attention?" widget="sort-buckets" config={vpValuesBuckets} />
  <p>
    Stories come from three broad currents. <strong>Enterprise</strong> stories come from
    the reporter's own ideas: reading a council agenda, noticing a pattern, following up a
    tip. <strong>Event</strong> stories come from scheduled happenings: courts,
    parliaments, press conferences, sports. <strong>Reactive</strong> stories respond to
    the world: a flood, a leak, a viral video. Crucially, sources talk to journalists for
    a reason — politicians want coverage, companies want favour, whistle-blowers want
    change. Assessing a story therefore includes assessing the <em>storyteller's motive</em>:
    who benefits from this being published, and what does this source get out of it?
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> many people assume journalists simply report "the facts".
  Selection is unavoidable. Out of thousands of events each day, a newsroom prints twenty.
  The professional duty is not to have no perspective — that is impossible — but to keep
  the <em>facts</em> and the <em>viewpoint</em> in separate, clearly labelled rooms.</p>

  <h2>The information lifecycle: why first reports get it wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a fact that explains half of all newsroom errors: a piece of information cannot
    exist until enough time has passed to produce it. A social media post can appear within
    seconds of an event; a daily newspaper within hours; a magazine piece within weeks; a
    carefully researched book takes years. This sequence is called the <strong>information
    lifecycle</strong> — the stages a story passes through as different formats become
    possible. Each stage has different time to check, so each stage has a different error
    rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequence is uncomfortable: sources made <em>later</em> in the lifecycle often
    reach more accurate conclusions than sources made early, precisely because they had
    time to verify. The clearest documented example is the Columbine High School shooting
    of 1999. Early news coverage concluded that the two students responsible had been
    shunned by classmates, and that revenge for exclusion drove the attack — and schools
    across the United States built programmes against exclusion on that basis. Book-length
    reporting, produced over the following years, found the opposite: the two were not
    shunned (one had attended prom activities days before), and mental illness was central.
    The early story was not a lie; it was an unverified story that arrived first and spread.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="rebuild the information lifecycle" widget="order" config={vpLifecycleOrder} />
  <p>
    The professional response is not to refuse to publish until the books are written —
    daily journalism would be impossible. It is to label the lifecycle stage: "police say",
    "unconfirmed", "early reports suggest". The reader's matching habit is to ask, of any
    story: how long after the event was this made, and what could <em>not</em> yet have
    been known when it was written?
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blanks — labelling the lifecycle stage" widget="cloze" config={vpStageCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> stories are selected by news values and coloured by source
    motives, and every story carries a timestamp in the information lifecycle that tells
    you how much checking it could have had. Now we look at the form that carries it.
  </p>

  <h2>News writing and reporting: the inverted pyramid</h2>
  <p>
    The dominant news form is the <strong>inverted pyramid</strong>: the most important
    information first — who, what, where, when, why and how — followed by supporting
    details, then background. It was reportedly forged on the Civil War telegraph wires,
    where a transmission could die at any moment and the dispatch had to carry its cargo in
    descending order of importance. That design still fits the modern web perfectly:
    readers skim, editors cut from the bottom, and search engines weight the opening lines.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pyramid is also a verification instrument. Writing "the most newsworthy fact first"
    forces the reporter to rank claims by confidence and consequence, and ranking exposes
    weakness. If the lead must say "police said a fire killed three people", that
    attribution signals the claim's provenance: police are the source, death tolls in the
    first hours are provisional, and a reporter who writes "three people died in a fire"
    flatly has borrowed more certainty than they own. Good attribution is honesty about
    knowledge — "according to a person briefed on the matter" tells you exactly how strong
    the ground is, and that the journalist is telling the truth about their footing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line — the lead rewrite" widget="spot-error" config={vpLeadSpot} />
  <p>
    The related genre boundary matters for verification too. <strong>News</strong> reports
    what happened and is verifiable. <strong>Opinion</strong> argues from values and is
    labelled. <strong>Analysis</strong> explains why, using interpretation that is
    disclosed. A claim verified to appear in news can be quoted; an opinion can be quoted
    <em>as an opinion</em> but never laundered into a fact. Confusing the three is how
    trust dies.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each genre with its rule" widget="match" config={vpGenreMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> readers sometimes think the pyramid exists "for people
  who skim". That is a side benefit, not the point. It exists because writing the ending
  first is a truth test: you cannot lead with a claim you have not verified, and the
  discipline of the lead is what catches unverified claims before they reach page two.</p>

  <h2>Interviewing and sourcing: the human side of checking</h2>
  <p>
    Most of what journalism knows enters through interviews, and every interview is
    simultaneously a data-collection exercise and a verification exercise. The craft rules
    exist to keep both honest. Prepare so thoroughly you can recognise a wrong answer. Ask
    open questions — who, what, why, how — and then shut up; silence is the most reliable
    follow-up question ever invented. Keep questions short, one at a time, and end with
    "what have I not asked that I should have?" Interviewees rarely volunteer damaging
    context unprompted, but many will share it if invited.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interviews come in flavours with different verification weights. An
    <strong>on-the-record</strong> quote can be published with attribution. <strong>On
    background</strong> ("a defence official") shapes the story without naming its origin.
    <strong>Off the record</strong> information cannot be published from that source at all
    — it can only point where to look. A professional keeps these states straight, because
    a slipped agreement destroys a source's career and the journalist's access. Anonymity
    is a bargain granted only when the information is vital, unobtainable on the record,
    and the editor knows who the source is; a paper that grants anonymity freely is
    selling its credibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two-source rule lives here. One source can be wrong, biased or playing you; the
    second source must be genuinely <em>independent</em> — not a colleague who heard the
    same briefing, not the spokesperson of the same department. Two eyewitnesses to the
    same crash count less than one eyewitness plus the traffic-camera file. Verification is
    triangulation: each independent line of evidence should reach the claim from a
    different direction.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "two sources said the same thing, so it is confirmed."
  It feels like confirmation because agreement looks like corroboration. But two sources
  that share an origin — the same briefing, the same document, the same rumour — are one
  source wearing two coats, and they can be wrong the same way. The test is not the number
  of voices; it is the number of independent routes the information took to reach you.
  One eyewitness plus a camera file beats two eyewitnesses who compared notes.</p>

  <h2>Feature writing: the long form</h2>
  <p>
    Not everything is a 300-word wire piece. A <strong>feature</strong> is the longer,
    free-form sibling of news: it can open with a scene, follow a person, explain a system
    or investigate an institution. Features still carry the verification burden — often a
    heavier one, because a feature stakes narrative authority rather than the speed
    authority of news. A news report claims "this happened"; a feature claims "I understand
    this", and readers forgive a slow start but not a fabricated detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    Features have their own sub-genres with different verification loads. A
    <strong>profile</strong> needs multiple interviews plus the subject's paper trail. An
    <strong>explainer</strong> needs expert review: the journalist's understanding must
    survive a specialist reading it. An <strong>investigation</strong> is essentially one
    long verification exercise — documents, data, interviews and legal review converging
    before a word is published, because an investigative story that misses can end in a
    libel judgment. The writing craft — scene, character, structure — is real work, but
    every scene must be sourced. "Reconstructed dialogue" that nobody attests to is
    fiction wearing journalism's clothes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file the sub-genres — whose verification load?" widget="sort-buckets" config={vpFeatureBuckets} />

  <h2>Sub-editing and editorial decision-making: the last gate</h2>
  <p>
    Before publication, copy passes through the <strong>sub-editor</strong>, whose job is
    far more than fixing typos. Subs check facts against the story's own sourcing, cut to
    fit, write headlines that the body text can support, check legal risk (defamation,
    contempt, privacy), and apply the style guide for consistency. A headline is a claim
    too: a body that says "police are investigating whether…" cannot honestly sit under a
    headline saying "minister stole funds". Clickbait mismatches are not just taste
    failures; they are verification failures.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the publish-or-hold decision" widget="branch" config={vpGateBranch} />
  <p>
    Above the subs sit editors making the publish-or-hold decision. The classic calculus
    weighs confidence, consequence and speed. Publishing a wrong report of a death is a
    catastrophic, irreversible harm; holding a true exclusive for an hour costs little.
    When a story is competitive and unconfirmed, the professional move is to publish what
    you <em>can</em> stand behind — "a powerful explosion was heard; authorities have not
    confirmed casualties" — rather than the unconfirmed claim itself. Editing is the
    institutional memory of these trade-offs, and a good editor is the person empowered to
    say no to the newsroom's own enthusiasm.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a correction means the paper failed." Actually, the
  opposite signal is usually true. Every serious newsroom errs; the trustworthy ones
  correct visibly, explain what went wrong, and log the correction prominently. A site
  with no corrections is not a site that is always right — it is a site that never tells
  you when it was wrong.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A rumour about a local school closure is spreading online. What are the first two checks you would run, and why in that order?</li>
    <li>A story written four hours after a fire says "three people died". A book written three years later says "two people died, and the first hours of reporting overstated the toll". Which is more likely to be accurate, and why?</li>
    <li>Your editor says a claim is "confirmed — two sources say it". What question do you ask next?</li>
    <li>A headline reads "minister stole funds" but the story says "police are investigating whether the minister misused funds". Which craft failure is this?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Find the original source (does the school itself say
    anything? — cheapest check, kills most rumours alone), then look for a second,
    independent confirmation (has any outlet or official spoken separately?). Cheapest and
    most decisive first, exactly as the five checks run. (2) Probably the book — it sits
    later in the information lifecycle and had years to verify, as the Columbine reporting
    showed. But check its sourcing too: late is a better bet, not a guarantee. (3) "Are
    they truly independent?" — two sources with one origin (same briefing, same document)
    can be wrong the same way. (4) A sub-editing failure — the headline claims more than
    the body can support. Headlines are claims and must be verified against the story
    under them.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Journalism's product is not the claim but the chain of checks behind it; verification happens before publication, because a correction never reaches everyone the original claim did.</li>
    <li>News values (impact, proximity, prominence, timeliness, conflict, novelty, human interest) decide what gets covered; source motives decide what to distrust.</li>
    <li>The information lifecycle explains why first reports err: a format cannot exist until time allows, and later formats usually had more time to check.</li>
    <li>The inverted pyramid is a truth test — leading with the top claim forces confidence ranking; attribution states honestly how strong the ground is.</li>
    <li>The two-source rule counts independence, not voices: one eyewitness plus a camera file beats two witnesses who compared notes.</li>
    <li>Sub-editors check facts, headlines and legal risk; editors weigh confidence, consequence and speed — and visible corrections are a mark of trust, not failure.</li>
    <li>Readers can run the same method: find the original, check independence, look for the source's motive, and ask what a correction would look like.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson gave you the whole method at walking pace. The next lessons slow down and
    sharpen parts of it: how to tell news, opinion and analysis apart; how the inverted
    pyramid works as a writing craft; how to plan and conduct a real interview; and, much
    later, how investigations combine documents, data and law. From there the subject
    builds outward into ethics and media law — the rules that make the checks enforceable.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Everything so far has been the newsroom's view. The reader's version has been
    condensed by the digital literacy researcher Mike Caulfield into four moves, known as
    the <strong>SIFT method</strong>. <strong>Stop</strong> when you meet a claim: do you
    know and trust the source, and are you being emotionally hijacked — social platforms
    deliberately promote outrage because outrage holds attention? <strong>Investigate the
    source</strong> — but <em>laterally</em>: professional fact-checkers do not read deeper
    into the site in front of them; they open new tabs and see what <em>others</em> say
    about it. <strong>Find better coverage</strong>: if the source is low quality or
    unknowable, trade up — you care about the claim, not the source that happened to carry
    it. <strong>Trace claims, quotes and media to their original context</strong>: the
    clipped video, the misleading caption, the study that "shows" something its abstract
    never said. Four moves, each taking minutes, covering most of what verification does.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two deeper threads are worth pulling. The first is <strong>economics</strong>:
    verification is expensive — a single investigative story can consume months of a
    reporter's salary — while a rumour is free to publish. This asymmetry explains the
    modern information disorder: rumour outruns truth for the same reason counterfeit
    money outruns the mint. Structural responses exist — wire services as shared
    verification infrastructure, news agencies' fact-check desks, independent
    fact-checking organisations, and provenance standards like C2PA, which
    cryptographically attaches capture data to images so a "real" photo carries its own
    receipt.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second is <strong>prebunking and literacy</strong>. Research on misinformation
    suggests that corrections after the fact are less effective than inoculation before
    it: teaching people to recognise the standard manipulation techniques (fear, outrage,
    false urgency) measurably reduces susceptibility. There are honest open problems here
    too. Generative AI makes fabricated text, images and video cheap and fluent, which
    raises the value of document-based and on-the-ground verification while devaluing "it
    looked real" as a test. And the two-source rule itself has limits: two sources can be
    wrong the same way when they share a defective origin — a trap that contributed to
    famous intelligence failures on Iraqi weapons before 2003. Verification is a
    discipline, not a spell; it lowers the probability of error, and honest journalism
    says so.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SPJ Code of Ethics; Kovach &amp; Rosenstiel, The Elements of Journalism; NCTJ Essential Journalism syllabus; BBC and AP verification guidelines; C2PA provenance specification; prebunking research (Roozenbeek &amp; van der Linden); LibreTexts, Gruwell &amp; Ewing, <em>Critical Thinking in Academic Research</em> (information lifecycle) and Butler, Sargent &amp; Smith, <em>Introduction to College Research</em> (SIFT), CC BY / CC BY-SA 4.0. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

</TopicPage>
